  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nCurrently, ridging dominates much of the country with a few \nshortwaves beginning to dig into the northern tier of the CONUS. At \nthe surface, a low pressure is centered over Ontario with a cold \nfront draped across the Northern Plains. Mid-level heights continue \nto increase across the region this afternoon leading to slightly \nwarmer temperatures compared to yesterday. Temperatures today will \nrange from 98-101, with many locations potentially seeing their \nfirst 100 degree reading of the year. A relatively moist airmass \nremains over the area with 1.50-1.75\" PWATs extending across the \neastern half of the state this afternoon leading to dewpoints in the \nlower 70s. Heat indices for much of the forecast area have already \nexceeded 100 degrees, and the Heat Advisory remains in effect till \n9pm this evening with heat index values around 105 degrees expected \nareawide. \n\nMoving into Tuesday, the trough currently situated over the Canadian \nPrairies will dig into the Great Lakes region. This will drag the \ncold frontal boundary south across the Central Plains through the \nmorning hours on Tuesday, shunting the ridge axis further south and \nwest. Winds will begin turning out of the north for out central \nKansas counties around 12Z. The front will traverse the entire \nforecast area by 18Z with northeasterly winds expected areawide. \nCooler and drier air will lag behind the frontal boundary, with \nafternoon highs still managing to reach into the mid 90s for central \nKansas locations and between 100-104 for our far southern counties. \nDewpoints for the southern half of the forecast area will still \nreach into the 70s for much of the afternoon, leading to dangerously \nhigh heat indices ranging from 105 to around 110 degrees. An Extreme \nHeat Warning has been issued for southern and southeast Kansas from \n12-8pm on Tuesday and a Heat Advisory has been issued for portions \nof south-central and southeast Kansas. \n\nFor Wednesday and Thursday, the forecast area will remain on the \nnortheast edge of the ridge with the ridge axis extending from the \nSouthern Plains northwestward into the Northern Rockies. \nTemperatures for mid week will top out near normal with highs in the \nupper 80s to lower 90s. Drier air behind the front will also make \nthings feel more comfortable as dewpoints in the upper 50s to lower \n60s are anticipated. A shortwave trough will begin to dig into the \nHigh Plains on Wednesday, traversing the Central Plains on Thursday \ninto Thursday night. This feature, along with a strengthening LLJ \neach night, will support rain and storm chances Wed. and Thu. nights \nfrom northern into eastern Kansas. \n\nThe ridge will begin to build back over the region on Friday, with \nincreasing mid-level heights through Saturday. The ridge axis looks \nto be situated just to our west for Sunday. Temperatures for Friday \nthrough the end of the forecast period look to gradually increase \neach day, with 100s possible again by the end of next weekend. With \nthe ridge nearly overhead for the end of the forecast time frame, \nnegligible rain chances and mostly clear skies are expected to \nreturn.",
